{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790168598,"nonce":"W6WNF7f8ToiKJDautbbQlkBAwTY=","title":"Found while implementing Caribou plugin enums and checking allocation overhead.","message":"std/src/types.rs::hlp_alloc_enum allocates size_of::\u003cvenum\u003e() + construct.size, but construct.size already includes the enum header. Both crates/ash/src/bytecode.rs::enum_construct_layout and hlp_init_enum start the size at pointer_size + sizeof(i32), then add aligned fields. A 32-byte constructor therefore requests 48 bytes on 64-bit hosts.\n\nThe explicit zeroing in hlp_alloc_enum and hlp_alloc_enum_dyn also treats construct.size as a payload-only length. Fixing the allocation without correcting those writes would cause an overrun. rt::gc_alloc already returns zeroed memory.\n\nFix: allocate the recorded total size (at least the Rust venum header size), remove the redundant payload zeroing, and validate type/constructor bounds before dereferencing. Add tests for exact allocation accounting, zeroed fields, nullary constructors and invalid indices, then run Caribou's mixed/nested enum fixture under GC stress.","files":null}]}